//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Articulation Point

Given an undirected graph with V vertices. The graph is represented as a 2D array edges[][], where each element edges[i] = [u, v] indicates an undirected edge between vertices u and v.
Return all the articulation points (or cut vertices) in the graph. 
An articulation point is a vertex whose removal, along with all its connected edges, increases the number of connected components in the graph.
The given graph may be disconnected, i.e., it may consist of more than one connected component.
If no such point exists, return {-1}.

Examples :

=======================================================================

Input: V = 5, edges[][] = [[0, 1], [1, 4], [4, 3], [4, 2], [2, 3]]

Output: [1, 4]

        0
        |
        1        <- remove 1
        |
        4        <- remove 4
       / \
      3---2

Removing vertex 1 splits {0} away from the rest.
Removing vertex 4 splits {0,1} away from {2,3}.

=======================================================================

Input: V = 4, edges[][] = [[0, 1], [0, 2]]

Output: [0]

        0        <- remove 0
       / \
      1   2

Removing vertex 0 leaves 1 and 2 alone, so components become 3.

Constraints:

1 <= V, edges.size() <= 10^4
0 <= edges[i][j] < V
edges[i].size() = 2
